{
  "esm": "1.1.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "ntiedtke_moist_thermodynamics",
    "description": "The five mixed-phase thermodynamic functions of WRF's New Tiedtke scheme, the module functions at the foot of `phys/physics_mmm/cu_ntiedtke.F90` (WRF 4.8). Every moist calculation in the scheme -- the environmental saturation, the test parcel, the updraught condensation, the downdraught evaporation, the freezing of cloud water, the precipitation conversion -- goes through them, so they are factored into a template library that each component imports rather than restated.\n\nThe scheme's phase rule is a single smooth WEIGHT, `foealfa`, which is 1 above the freezing point, 0 at and below tmelt - 23 K, and the square of the linear ramp between. Everything else is that weight blended between a liquid and an ice branch: the latent heat (`foelhm`), the saturation vapour pressure (`foeewm`, two Magnus formulae), the Clausius-Clapeyron derivative scaled by L/cp (`foedem`), and the temperature rise per unit condensate (`foeldcpm`). Because ONE weight drives all four, a mixed-phase state is thermodynamically consistent by construction, and `foealfa` is written here once and applied by name in the other four.\n\nFREE-NAME CONTRACT. The bodies read the scheme's constants by the scoped names `par.tmelt`, `par.c2es`, `par.c3les`, `par.c3ies`, `par.c4les`, `par.c4ies`, `par.rtice`, `par.rtwat`, `par.r5alvcp`, `par.r5alscp`, `par.ralvdcp`, `par.ralsdcp`, `par.alv` and `par.als` -- that is, a consuming model MUST mount `ntiedtke_parameters.esm` under the key `par` and `lib/wrf_constants.esm` under `wrf`. It is the same keyed-factor convention the EarthSciDiscretizations column rules use for `dz`: a template library cannot mount a subsystem of its own, and threading fourteen constants through the parameter list of every call would drown the physics.\n\nThe argument is always a temperature and the functions are pointwise, so each applies elementwise to a shaped field as readily as to a scalar.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": ["wrf", "convection", "new-tiedtke", "thermodynamics", "mixed-phase", "expression-templates"],
    "references": [
      { "citation": "Tiedtke, M. (1989). A comprehensive mass flux scheme for cumulus parameterization in large-scale models. Mon. Wea. Rev. 117, 1779-1800.", "doi": "10.1175/1520-0493(1989)117<1779:ACMFSF>2.0.CO;2" },
      { "citation": "NCAR/MMM-physics cu_ntiedtke.F90 (WRF 4.8 phys/physics_mmm submodule), functions foealfa, foelhm, foeewm, foedem, foeldcpm." }
    ]
  },
  "expression_templates": {
    "foealfa": {
      "params": ["tt"],
      "description": "Liquid fraction of the condensate at temperature tt: 1 for pure water at and above rtwat = tmelt, 0 for pure ice at and below rtice = tmelt - 23 K, and the SQUARE of the linear ramp in between, additionally capped at 1. The square makes the transition tangent to the ice branch at the cold end, so the phase weight has no kink there. The outer `min(1., ...)` is redundant given the inner clamp -- the ramp cannot exceed 1 once tt is clipped to [rtice, rtwat] -- and is transcribed because the Fortran writes it.",
      "body": {
        "op": "min", "args": [1.0,
          { "op": "^", "args": [
            { "op": "/", "args": [
              { "op": "-", "args": [
                { "op": "max", "args": ["par.rtice", { "op": "min", "args": ["par.rtwat", "tt"] }] },
                "par.rtice"] },
              { "op": "-", "args": ["par.rtwat", "par.rtice"] }] },
            2] }]
      }
    },
    "foelhm": {
      "params": ["tt"],
      "description": "Effective latent heat at temperature tt, the liquid fraction blend of the latent heat of vaporisation and of sublimation.",
      "body": {
        "op": "+", "args": [
          { "op": "*", "args": [
            { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "foealfa", "bindings": { "tt": "tt" } },
            "wrf.xlv"] },
          { "op": "*", "args": [
            { "op": "-", "args": [1.0,
              { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "foealfa", "bindings": { "tt": "tt" } }] },
            "wrf.xls"] }]
      }
    },
    "foeewm": {
      "params": ["tt"],
      "description": "Saturation vapour pressure at temperature tt, already multiplied by R_d/R_v so that dividing by the pressure gives a saturation MIXING RATIO directly: c2es times the liquid-fraction blend of the two Magnus formulae, exp(c3les (T - tmelt)/(T - c4les)) over water and exp(c3ies (T - tmelt)/(T - c4ies)) over ice. Its constants are the ones FORTRAN_BUGS N109 is about: tmelt and the four Magnus coefficients are default-real literals, and carrying their decimals instead of their binary32 roundings misses the kernel by 2.6e-7 relative.",
      "body": {
        "op": "*", "args": ["par.c2es",
          { "op": "+", "args": [
            { "op": "*", "args": [
              { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "foealfa", "bindings": { "tt": "tt" } },
              { "op": "exp", "args": [{ "op": "/", "args": [
                { "op": "*", "args": ["par.c3les", { "op": "-", "args": ["tt", "par.tmelt"] }] },
                { "op": "-", "args": ["tt", "par.c4les"] }] }] }] },
            { "op": "*", "args": [
              { "op": "-", "args": [1.0,
                { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "foealfa", "bindings": { "tt": "tt" } }] },
              { "op": "exp", "args": [{ "op": "/", "args": [
                { "op": "*", "args": ["par.c3ies", { "op": "-", "args": ["tt", "par.tmelt"] }] },
                { "op": "-", "args": ["tt", "par.c4ies"] }] }] }] }] }]
      }
    },
    "foedem": {
      "params": ["tt"],
      "description": "Clausius-Clapeyron derivative of the saturation mixing ratio with respect to temperature, already scaled by L/cp: the liquid-fraction blend of r5alvcp/(T - c4les)^2 and r5alscp/(T - c4ies)^2. It is the denominator term of the Newton step in the moist adjustment -- the amount by which saturation moves when the latent heat released warms the parcel.",
      "body": {
        "op": "+", "args": [
          { "op": "*", "args": [
            { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "foealfa", "bindings": { "tt": "tt" } },
            "par.r5alvcp",
            { "op": "/", "args": [1.0,
              { "op": "^", "args": [{ "op": "-", "args": ["tt", "par.c4les"] }, 2] }] }] },
          { "op": "*", "args": [
            { "op": "-", "args": [1.0,
              { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "foealfa", "bindings": { "tt": "tt" } }] },
            "par.r5alscp",
            { "op": "/", "args": [1.0,
              { "op": "^", "args": [{ "op": "-", "args": ["tt", "par.c4ies"] }, 2] }] }] }]
      }
    },
    "foeldcpm": {
      "params": ["tt"],
      "description": "Temperature rise per unit condensed mixing ratio, L/cp, blended by the liquid fraction between L_v/cp and L_s/cp. It is `foelhm` divided by cp, written separately because that is how the scheme writes it.",
      "body": {
        "op": "+", "args": [
          { "op": "*", "args": [
            { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "foealfa", "bindings": { "tt": "tt" } },
            "par.ralvdcp"] },
          { "op": "*", "args": [
            { "op": "-", "args": [1.0,
              { "op": "apply_expression_template", "args": [], "name": "foealfa", "bindings": { "tt": "tt" } }] },
            "par.ralsdcp"] }]
      }
    }
  }
}
